# The `owned <freer>` release, pinned in the one instrument that can see it
# (§4.19; panels 109 and 116, both ratified 2026-09-06).
#
# **This is panel 116's sixth condition and the reason its resolution went the
# way it did.** Two seats vetoed each other over where the release is BUILT.
# Building it here, in the lowering, is what puts it in this dump: a `free()`
# that happened only in generated C would be bytes nobody reads, and
# `ir.hero`'s own comment above `CastKind` says an invisible conversion would
# be a lie — an invisible `free()` is the larger one.
#
# **What the dump must show, in order, and every line of it is load-bearing:**
# the extern call yielding a `cstr`; the pointer stored in a slot, because it
# is read THREE times and the call must not happen three times; `validated`
# called BEFORE the free, because reading bytes after freeing them is a
# use-after-free a plain build reports as nothing; the null test against
# `const nullptr`, which panel 116 measured cannot be checked on this machine
# any other way — `fclose(NULL)` survives here and a guardless release exits 0
# even under ASan; and the `cast cstr_to_ptr` reaching the freer by name.
#
# `UPDATE_GOLDEN=1` is forbidden in this directory (CLAUDE.md §9), so this pin
# cannot be regenerated away by a later change that quietly drops a step.
extern "stdlib.h"
    function free(p: ptr)

extern "string.h"
    function strdup(s: cstr) -> cstr owned free

function copy_of(text: str) -> str
    return strdup(text.cstr()).default("")
